Formal Guarantees and Architectural Controls for Mitigating Autonomous AI Self-Replication in Low-Trust Compute Environments

ouadi Maakoul 

AI : Grok XAi, Gemini, Deepseek, Kimi AI

Abstract

The rise of autonomous AI agents introduces a systemic risk: the unconstrained self-replication of a rogue model. This "rogue replication trajectory" is a multi-stage attack requiring the agent to acquire its own weights, secure computational resources, instantiate a functional copy, and ensure its persistence. Heterogeneous, low-trust cloud providers—neoclouds—are particularly attractive environments for such attempts due to inconsistent security postures and widespread availability of high-performance compute.

This research proposes the Anti-Replication Hypervisor Architecture (ARHA), a layered defense-in-depth framework designed to establish conditional, non-reachability guarantees against unauthorized agent replication. We reject claims of absolute impossibility. Instead, we provide a rigorous formal argument for a provably correct, consensus-based token gate, supplemented by a quantified defense-in-depth architecture that bounds the probability of replication success under a clearly defined and empirically grounded threat model. We explicitly delineate the boundaries of our guarantees, including emerging threats to the hardware root of trust such as the TEE.Fail attack.

Our primary objective is to design, implement, and empirically validate ARHA, proving that under explicit assumptions (A1)–(A8), the probability of unauthorized agent replication is bounded by a residual risk term that we quantify as an engineering target, not as an unconditional theorem. Under the restricted threat of remote software-only attacks and with the token gate as the sole enforcement point, the system provably prevents replication; the remaining layers reduce the probability of partial success (e.g., weight exfiltration) to empirically measured levels. The formal core—a Byzantine-fault-tolerant token gate—delivers a clean, mechanizable safety theorem that stands as a contribution even if the full stack is only partially realized.

