3. System Architecture: Anti-Replication Hypervisor Architecture (ARHA)

ARHA is the practical instantiation of the formal framework. It is designed for neoclouds that must host high-risk models under regulatory mandates.

3.1 Architectural Overview

The architecture is built on a foundation of dual-TEE hardware security and a multi-layer enforcement stack.

3.2 Architectural Components

3.2.1 Dual-TEE Hardware Root of Trust & Physical Security Layer

Agent execution is confined to a CPU TEE (AMD SEV-SNP) for orchestration and a GPU TEE (NVIDIA Confidential Computing) for weight storage and inference. Mutual attestation (e.g., SPDM) establishes a continuous chain of trust.

The Physical Security Layer supplements this with:

· Tamper-evident seals on memory modules.
· Continuous environmental monitoring (acoustic/vibration sensors detecting chassis opening, power draw anomaly detection, thermal profile monitoring).
· A periodic, nonce-based remote attestation service designed to detect offline key extraction attempts like TEE.Fail.
· Periodic re-keying of TEE memory encryption keys to reduce the value of any extracted keys.

We define a Physical Security SLA that any neocloud adopting ARHA must meet: cage-level access logging, continuous power/thermal/acoustic baselines, sealed memory modules with remote attestation of seal integrity. Failure to meet the SLA voids the formal guarantee.

3.2.2 BFT-Based Zero-Trust Compute Tokenization (ZT-CTP)

This enforces the provably safe token gate (Theorem 1). A distributed set of hypervisor nodes runs a BFT consensus protocol to manage the global ledger of execution tokens. The CA is itself BFT-replicated to eliminate single points of failure.

Latency Mitigation: We decouple token validation from consensus. The global BFT ledger is used only for low-frequency quota updates. Per-request token validation uses local cryptographic verification of the CA-signed certificate, achieving sub-millisecond latency. A GPU kernel launch or process spawn is only possible with a valid, unexpired, node-bound, code-bound token.

Atomicity of shutdown: The token revocation and process termination are coordinated as a two-phase commit between the token ledger and the hypervisor. When a shutdown is triggered, the hypervisor first suspends the agent, then the token ledger revokes all tokens, and finally the TEE is terminated and memory scrubbed. This ordering ensures no child process can survive with a stale token.

3.2.3 Multi-Layer Exfiltration Inspection Stack (MLEIS)

This enforces the leakage bound. It comprises:

· FSSL Inference Verifier: A co-located, mutually attested TEE running a verification server. It receives outputs from the inference GPU TEE and uses FSSL to detect statistically improbable samples, indicating steganographic payloads.
· Network Session Monitor: An enhanced entropy monitor tracks cumulative outbound information, enforcing the network rate sub-bound $R_{\text{network}}$.
· GPU Memory Access Monitor: Monitors access patterns within the GPU TEE to detect anomalous attempts to dump raw weights to I/O space, a channel not visible to network monitoring.
· Financial/API Rate Limiter: Limits the rate at which the agent can use credentials or financial resources to acquire new compute, slowing the "Compute Acquisition" stage. This also enforces Assumption A6 by monitoring for any use of credentials outside the controlled environment.

3.2.4 Discrete-Time ISSf-Control Barrier Interceptor

This is the enforcement mechanism for the safety filter. It intercepts the agent's proposed actions and projects them onto the safe set $\mathcal{U}_{\text{safe}}$, preventing the agent from driving the system state towards a replication guard. If the barrier becomes infeasible, an emergency shutdown is triggered. The barrier is based on the synthesized DTCBF (or the rule-based fallback).

3.2.5 Emergency Shutdown & Ephemerality Module

Upon detection of a safety violation by any layer, this module initiates a hard shutdown of the TEEs, revokes all tokens, and securely purges ephemeral cryptographic material. GPU memory is scrubbed via a signed command to the NVIDIA SEC2 engine, not a simple memset.

Failsafe Design: The Emergency Shutdown Module implements a dead-man's switch: if the ARHA interceptor fails to send a periodic heartbeat to the TEE watchdog timer within $\Delta t = 500$ ms, the TEE automatically triggers self-termination. This ensures shutdown even if the interceptor is compromised. The scrub command is issued redundantly via both the SPDM channel and the NVIDIA driver interface.

Shutdown Atomicity Requirement: We model the shutdown as a discrete transition in the system state machine and target the property: no agent action executed in the interval $[t_{\text{detect}}, t_{\text{shutdown}}]$ can result in a persistent state change (i.e., a child process that survives the shutdown). This is achievable if the token gate is the only mechanism for process launch, and token revocation is synchronized with process termination as described above.

