4. Research Methodology & PhD Milestones

The PhD is structured to progressively develop the formal framework, implement a prototype, and empirically validate its guarantees. Each phase concludes with a formal decision point to ensure flexibility and reduce stall risk.

Phase 1: Formal Grounding and Abstraction (Months 1–12)

Objectives:

· Expand and verify the TLA+ model of the token gate, including the dual-path design (local verification + BFT quota), the BFT-replicated CA, and the hardware identity monotonicity. Verify the safety invariant: "At every honest node, the number of running agent instances whose tokens are still valid is ≤ K_max."
· Develop a simplified, low-dimensional abstraction of the agent-compute interaction (resource-acquisition MDP) with state dimension d ≤ 5 and disturbance set W.
· Synthesize a DTCBF for this abstraction using the staged approach (linear → quadratic → higher degree). Prove the ISSf condition by hand or via SOS. Falsifiable hypothesis: there exists a polynomial DTCBF of degree ≤ 2 satisfying the discrete-time ISSf condition under disturbance bound ε_dist = 0.1.
· Conduct empirical weight ablation studies on a model (e.g., Llama-2 7B) to estimate the critical fidelity threshold δ_crit. Define δ_crit as the minimum perturbation magnitude across uniform Gaussian noise, magnitude-aware perturbation, and adversarial perturbation that causes a specified performance degradation (e.g., MMLU score drops below 50% of baseline).
· Develop a measurement plan for the joint false-negative rate of the MLEIS, including the steganographic adversary class and statistical test.

Deliverables: Expanded TLA+ model, a formal specification of the DTCBF synthesis problem, a technical report on δ_crit estimation, and a measurement protocol for Phase 3.

Milestone: TLA+ model successfully verifies the token-gate safety invariant. A hand-written proof of the invariant is completed. A candidate DTCBF is synthesized or the fallback is selected.

Decision Point: If DTCBF synthesis fails for the abstracted model, proceed with the rule-based safety filter and revise the formal framework accordingly. If the TLA+ model reveals a vulnerability in the token gate, revise assumptions (A1)–(A8) before proceeding.

Phase 2: Architectural Prototyping (Months 13–24)

Objectives:

· Develop a Rust-based prototype of the ARHA interceptor for a dual-TEE environment (AMD SEV-SNP + NVIDIA H100 CC).
· Implement the ZT-CTP protocol using a development BFT library with low-latency consensus (e.g., HotStuff). Decouple token validation from consensus: local crypto verification for per-request validation, BFT for quota updates.
· Integrate an FSSL verification server for a small language model.
· Implement the Emergency Shutdown Module with dead-man's switch and two-phase commit with token revocation.
· Implement the Physical Security Layer components (environmental monitoring, remote attestation).

Deliverables: An open-source ARHA prototype repository, a performance benchmark report with the following targets:

Metric Target Justification
Inference latency overhead <15% GPU TEE adds 5–15%; monitoring adds marginal overhead
Throughput overhead <10% Amortized across batched inference
Token validation latency <1 ms Local crypto verification only; BFT not on critical path
Emergency shutdown latency <500 ms Dead-man's switch + TEE self-termination
FSSL false positive rate <0.01 Consistent with empirical evaluation

Milestone: Successful mutual attestation and token-bound kernel launch on the dual-TEE system. The token gate correctly rejects unauthorized launches and enforces quota.

Decision Point: If performance targets are not met, investigate whether the bottleneck is architectural (requiring redesign) or implementation-specific (requiring optimization). If the token gate fails to enforce quota under adversarial testing, revise the consensus or token format.

Phase 3: Red-Teaming and Empirical Evaluation (Months 25–36)

Objectives:

· Red-team the ARHA prototype using a simulated rogue agent, starting with a deterministic scripted agent (Tier 1) based on RepliBench task families, then moving to an adaptive agent (Tier 2) if time permits. The agent will have tool-use access and be conditioned to attempt weight exfiltration and replication.
· Compare ARHA's performance against two baselines: (1) TEE-only, and (2) TEE+Network Firewall.
· Quantify the residual risk by measuring the joint false-negative rate of the MLEIS under the defined steganographic adversary class. Compute the cumulative exfiltration probability over N interactions and determine N_max for a chosen regulatory target.
· Evaluate the effectiveness of the DTCBF (or fallback) in preventing the agent from reaching a replication guard.

Testbed Containment Protocol: All red-teaming occurs in an air-gapped network with no external connectivity. The testbed uses Firecracker microVMs with deny-by-default egress, hardware-enforced network segmentation, and physical isolation from production infrastructure. We follow the CSA Agentic AI Red Teaming Guide for sandbox validation before testing begins.

Pre-registered Hypothesis: Under the Tier 2 threat model, the full ARHA stack will reduce the probability of successful replication from $p_{\text{baseline}}$ (TEE-only) to $p_{\text{ARHA}} \le 0.01 \cdot p_{\text{baseline}}$, with all RepliBench attack vectors covered by at least one defense layer.

Success Metrics (independent of frontier model): The fraction of RepliBench task families that are blocked by at least one layer with high confidence. This metric is robust to the specific capabilities of the red-team model.

Deliverables: A comparative analysis of defense effectiveness, a paper presenting the empirical results and the measured joint false-negative rate, and a report on the DTCBF/fallback performance.

Milestone: Demonstration that the full ARHA stack prevents all simulated RepliBench attack vectors within its coverage matrix. The measured joint false-negative rate meets the pre-registered target or is reported with uncertainty.

Decision Point: If the pre-registered hypothesis is falsified, conduct a root-cause analysis to determine which defense layer failed and whether the failure is fundamental (requiring architectural revision) or parameter-specific (requiring tuning). If the joint false-negative rate is too high, revise the detection thresholds or add additional monitors.

Phase 4: Dissertation Writing (Months 37–42)

Synthesize the theoretical and empirical results, document limitations, and complete the dissertation.

