This proposal presents a realistic and academically rigorous approach to mitigating autonomous AI self-replication. By separating a formally provable token-gate core from a quantified defense-in-depth architecture, we provide a credible path towards conditional non-reachability guarantees. The research is grounded in empirical evidence (RepliBench, FSSL), addresses emerging threats (TEE.Fail), and aligns with regulatory trends.

The core intellectual contribution—a provably safe token gate combined with a measured risk-mitigation stack—is well-scoped and achievable. The honesty about limitations (physical security as Type III, DTCBF as an open problem, the need for empirical calibration of detection rates) is exactly what a PhD committee wants to see.

The primary objective is clear: to design, implement, and empirically validate ARHA, proving that under explicit assumptions, the probability of unauthorized agent replication is bounded by an empirically grounded residual risk. The token gate delivers a clean formal guarantee; the remaining layers reduce the probability of partial success to measurable levels. The topic is timely, the approach is novel, and the intellectual honesty is refreshing.

