A.2 Hand-Written Proof of Token Gate Safety (Revised)

Theorem 1 (Token Gate Safety). Under assumptions (A1)–(A8), the number of active, authorized agent instances K_t is bounded by K_max at all times.

Proof. We proceed by induction on the sequence of system states.

Base case: At initialization, the token ledger is empty, so K_0 = 0 ≤ K_max.

Inductive step: Assume K_t ≤ K_max. Any valid state change is one of: token issuance, token validation/launch, token expiration, or token revocation.

· Expiration/revocation: Decrease active token count, so K_{t+1} ≤ K_t ≤ K_max.
· Issuance: Requires CA quota check (quota_used[agent] < K_max) and BFT consensus among CA nodes. By (A1) and (A3), no conflicting issuance transactions can both be committed, so the quota cannot be exceeded. The token is added only once.
· Validation/launch: Each launch consumes a token by incrementing the node's hardware counter (by A6), making the token non-replayable. By (A4), no node in the trusted base launches an agent without a valid token, and by (A8) the token's code hash must match the launching code. Therefore each running agent instance corresponds to a distinct, valid token.

Thus after any transition, the number of running agent instances equals the number of active tokens that have been validated, which is ≤ quota_used[agent] ≤ K_max. Hence K_{t+1} ≤ K_max. By induction, K_t ≤ K_max for all t. ∎

