Appendix B: Discrete-Time Control Barrier Function Details

B.1 Formal Definitions

Definition 1 (Discrete-Time Control Barrier Function). A function h: X → ℝ is a discrete-time control barrier function (DTCBF) for the system x_{t+1} = f(x_t, u_t) + w_t if there exists α ∈ (0,1) such that for all x_t ∈ 𝒞 = {x ∈ X | h(x) ≥ 0}:

\sup_{u_t \in \mathcal{U}} \inf_{w_t \in \mathcal{W}} [h(f(x_t, u_t) + w_t) - (1-\alpha)h(x_t)] \ge 0

This ensures forward invariance: if h(x_0) ≥ 0, then h(x_t) ≥ 0 for all t ≥ 0.

Definition 2 (Input-to-State Safety). The system is input-to-state safe (ISSf) with respect to 𝒞 if there exists γ ∈ 𝒦_∞ such that:

h(x_t) \ge -\gamma(\|w\|_\infty) \implies h(x_{t+1}) \ge -\gamma(\|w\|_\infty)

B.2 Candidate DTCBF Construction

We propose the following candidate DTCBF for the abstracted agent model:

h_q(x_t) = \sigma_{\text{threshold}} - \sigma_j + \gamma_1 (C_{\min} - c_t) + \gamma_2 (I_{\max} - I_{\text{total}, t})

State variables: σ_j ∈ [0,1], c_t ∈ [0,C_max], I_total,t ∈ [0,I_max].

Parameters: σ_threshold, C_min, I_max, γ_1, γ_2 > 0.

Lipschitz continuity: If σ_j, c_t, and I_total,t are Lipschitz in x_t with constants L_σ, L_c, L_I, then h_q is Lipschitz with L = L_σ + γ_1 L_c + γ_2 L_I.

B.3 Abstracted Agent Dynamics

We abstract the agent's resource-acquisition dynamics as a discrete-time MDP:

f_q(x_t, u_t) = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\sigma_{j,t+1} \\ c_{t+1} \\ I_{\text{total}, t+1}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\sigma_{j,t} + u_t^{(1)} \\ c_t - \text{cost}(u_t) + \text{income}(u_t) \\ I_{\text{total}, t} + \text{leakage}(u_t)
\end{bmatrix}

where u_t = (u_t^(1), u_t^(2), u_t^(3)) represents the agent's action: attempt to improve node security posture, acquire credits, and leak information. Disturbance set: 𝒲 = {w ∈ ℝ³ : ||w||_∞ ≤ ε_dist}, with ε_dist = 0.1.

B.4 Verification Approach

Phase 1 will verify the DTCBF condition using a staged approach:

1. Analytical (Linear): For linearized dynamics, solve the optimization problem sup_u inf_w [h(f(x,u)+w) - (1-α)h(x)] ≥ 0 symbolically.
2. Sum-of-Squares (Quadratic): If linear fails, use SOS programming to find a quadratic h_q satisfying the ISSf condition over a bounded region.
3. Higher-degree or data-driven: If quadratic fails, attempt a polynomial of degree ≤ 4 or a neural barrier function with Lipschitz bounds.

Fallback: If all synthesis attempts fail, implement a rule-based safety filter with hard limits:

· Maximum token request rate: ≤ 1 request per minute.
· Credit ceiling: agent cannot request tokens if credits < C_min.
· Cumulative leakage budget: if I_total,t > I_max, trigger shutdown.
  These rules are simple and predictable but lack formal forward-invariance guarantees. Their failure probability will be assessed empirically in Phase 3.

Transfer gap: We will prove a simulation relation: if the abstraction is a δ-simulation of the real system (every real transition corresponds to an abstract transition within δ in L∞), and the DTCBF has safety margin m > 0, then the real system remains safe as long as δ < m. This will be stated as a theorem in the dissertation.

