Appendix C: FSSL Verification Server Details

C.1 Fixed-Seed Sampling Likelihood (FSSL) Protocol

The FSSL protocol verifies that LLM inference is performed on the intended weights rather than a tampered or compressed version.

Protocol:

1. Setup: Verifier selects a fixed seed s and sends it to the prover (inference TEE).
2. Sampling: Prover generates outputs using seeded RNG, producing tokens y_1, ..., y_n.
3. Verification: Verifier computes likelihood of outputs under expected weight distribution. If likelihood < τ, reject.

Theoretical guarantee: If the prover uses weights differing by more than δ, the probability of passing verification is ≤ ε_FSSL, where ε_FSSL is a function of n and τ. The FSSL paper reports <0.5% exfiltration at <0.01 false positive rate.

C.2 Integration with ARHA

In ARHA, the FSSL verification server runs in a co-located, mutually attested TEE. Continuous verification samples outputs and checks against expected distribution. If statistically improbable samples are detected, an alert triggers the Emergency Shutdown Module. The FSSL verifier tracks cumulative leakage and enforces the inference leakage sub-bound R_inference.

C.3 False Negative Rate Analysis and Joint Detection

The individual false negative rate of FSSL is p_FN ≈ 0.01 under the tested conditions. However, the overall exfiltration detection uses multiple monitors jointly. The joint false negative rate p_FN,joint is the probability that all monitors (FSSL, network, GPU memory) simultaneously fail to detect an exfiltration attempt. This rate is not assumed to be the product of individual rates due to potential correlations. Instead, it will be measured empirically in Phase 3 under a defined steganographic adversary class.

The cumulative probability of at least one successful undetected exfiltration over N interactions is:

\epsilon_{\text{exfil}} = 1 - (1 - p_{\text{FN,joint}})^N

Given a regulatory target ε_exfil ≤ ε_target (e.g., 10⁻³), the maximum tolerable interaction count is:

N_{\max} = \left\lfloor \frac{\log(1 - \epsilon_{\text{target}})}{\log(1 - p_{\text{FN,joint}})} \right\rfloor

The ARHA interceptor will include a counter that triggers emergency shutdown when N approaches N_max, ensuring the cumulative risk remains bounded.

If the measured p_FN,joint is too high (e.g., 10⁻⁵), N_max will be small; additional monitors or tighter thresholds may be required. This is an open empirical question to be resolved in Phase 3.

